+## Development Cycle
+Use the `$tdd` skill for feature work and bug fixes. Work in small vertical slices:
+1. Add/update the relevant repo spec/change requirement or acceptance criteria.
+2. Add one failing behavior test through the public interface.
+3. Implement the minimum code needed to pass that test.
+4. Repeat for the next behavior.
+5. Refactor only after tests are green.
+6. Run relevant tests and update the active change evidence/tasks files when the repo spec workflow expects it.
+
+Tests should describe observable behavior and avoid coupling to implementation details.
+
+For feature/spec work, keep active `backend/sources/specs/changes/<change-id>/tasks.md` current enough that another agent can resume work. Update tasks for meaningful behavior changes; skip churn for tiny doc/config/mechanical edits.
+
+## Spec Change Policy
+If the user explicitly asks to create a new change, create one.
+
+If the user does not explicitly say whether to create a new change, decide from context:
+- If an open change is already being worked in the current session and request concerns same behavior, treat it as part of that change.
+- If there is no open change, create or extend a repo change under `backend/sources/specs/changes/` for behavior/requirement changes unless request is very small or not a spec change.
+- Documentation-only edits, deployment variable fixes, build plumbing, and similarly narrow maintenance work usually do not need a new spec change.
+- Bug fixes should still start with a failing test. Add required behavior as an addendum to the original spec/change that defined intended behavior, not an unrelated new requirement.
+
+When in doubt, make reasoning explicit before editing specs or code.
+
+## Change Completion
+A spec/change is not done just because code is merged, tests pass, or validation succeeds.
+
+A change is ready to accept/archive only after intended behavior is verified locally using Docker compose build/up.
+
+Use the most direct operational surface available:
+- relevant service APIs
+- application logs and deployment logs
+- backend/transport logs needed to prove end-to-end behavior
+
+When the current change modifies or adds a REST endpoint, route, middleware behavior affecting that route, or its downstream request handling, starting the containers and checking only a health endpoint is not sufficient. Send a representative REST request through the modified endpoint in the local Docker Compose environment and verify the expected HTTP status, content type, minimal response shape or invariant, and relevant application or downstream logs. If authentication or a dependency prevents the exact end-to-end request, use a deterministic local mock or fixture at that boundary and document any test-only deviation; do not claim runtime verification from health alone.
+
+State exactly why and what lower-level verification was performed.
+
+Use the shared `$change-accepted` skill for technical closeout. A current completion record from `$code-review` is required before declaring technical completion or archiving. Those skills own the acceptance timing, applicability, verification and review method; keep initial implementation readiness distinct from technical completion. Repository-specific standards and operational checks below remain applicable.
+
+## Commit / PR Guidelines
+Commits:
+- Use concise imperative subject: `<type>(<scope>): <summary>`.
+- Recommended types: `feat`, `fix`, `refactor`, `test`, `docs`, `chore`.
+- Keep first line <= 72 chars.
+- Reference the active spec/change id in body when applicable.
+
+Pull/Merge Requests:
+- Include sections: `Summary`, `OpenSpec Change`, `Test Evidence`, `Runtime Verification`, `Risks/Rollback`.
+- List exact commands run and their outcome.
+- Link affected spec/change files.
+- Keep PR focused to one behavior change set.
